{
  "beruf": "einzelhandel",
  "title": "Retail and supermarkets",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "italian-for-work run, 2026-11-15: written by hand for Italy, not yet read by a human or a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation. Working conditions are general orientation only; facts marked 'to verify' in the run report.",
  "intro": "Shops and supermarkets hire all year, and customer contact is the fastest way to practise Italian. This deck has the shop floor and the till, the daily jobs (stocking shelves, labels, the counter), the phrases for customers, returns and offers, security, and the words around shifts at the weekend and on public holidays. With customers you say Lei; with colleagues you say tu.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work in a shop or supermarket in Italy? Learn the words for the till, the shelves and your customers.",
  "jobs": [
    "Commesso/a",
    "Cassiere/a",
    "Addetto/a al reparto",
    "Scaffalista",
    "Addetto/a al banco salumi",
    "Responsabile di reparto"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "At the till customers hear the same few questions every day: «Passa la tessera?», «Sacchetto?», «Carta o contanti?». Learn them as blocks and say them without thinking.",
    "If you don't know something, say «Un attimo, chiedo al responsabile.» Nobody expects you to know every price, and asking is better than guessing.",
    "Shifts at the weekend and on public holidays (festivi) are common in shops. Whether you get extra pay (maggiorazione), a day of rest in return or a different rule depends on your contract and the CCNL (for example commercio). Ask the employer, a union (sindacato/patronato) or the Ispettorato del lavoro; this is general orientation, not legal advice.",
    "Always get your contract in writing and check the busta paga each month. Unpaid trial days or cash-in-hand work (lavoro in nero) are illegal; ask a union (sindacato/patronato) if you are unsure."
  ],
